WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.980
A secure backup script should not begin with a random list of directories.

2
00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:06.840
It should begin with a recovery need.

3
00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:08.860
What system must be restored?

4
00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:10.960
What data must be present?

5
00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:21.140
What configuration, metadata, keys, service definitions, schemas, package information, or dependency records are required for that restoration to work?

6
00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:28.280
A backup that captures many files but misses one critical dependency may still fail the organization when recovery is needed.

7
00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:30.720
Scope should be owned and reviewed.

8
00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:37.860
Teams should know which application, system, service, or business process the backup supports.

9
00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:41.560
Data classification then helps decide how the backup should be protected.

10
00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:51.020
Public documentation, internal configuration, regulated data, credentials, and production databases do not have the same handling requirements.

11
00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:59.920
Classification influences access control, encryption, storage location, retention, monitoring, and who may request restoration.

12
00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:07.240
Recovery point objectives and recovery time objectives translate business needs into technical decisions.

13
00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:12.540
A short recovery point objective may require more frequent backups or continuous replication.

14
00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:20.960
A short recovery time objective may require faster storage, documented restore procedures, and practiced recovery steps.

15
00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:23.220
These goals also shape alerting.

16
00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:29.880
A missed hourly backup is more urgent for one system than a missed weekly archive is for another.

17
00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:33.920
Clear exclusions are part of secure scope.

18
00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:40.260
Unnecessary data increases exposure, cost, retention complexity, and restore noise.

19
00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:48.920
Temporary build products, local caches, broad logs, and unrelated directories may not belong in a backup even if they are easy to include.

20
00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:58.700
The best scope maps directly to recovery: enough information to restore the service or process, with unnecessary sensitive or low-value data left out deliberately.
